<para/><item>Use the sim to model 6✕18. What is the total area of this rectangle? Represent the total area in multiple ways.</item>

<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fbcd2c0d72399bc1a9f08f144315632f87b9fc23"/></media></figure><para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 108. Using the area model, 6•18 = 6•10 + 6•8, which is 60+48. 

    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/53b13c4472c48e417696566d2d3c19aeb2c7236f"/></media></figure></para>

<para>
    </para><item>In an area model, how do the partial products (interior numbers) get calculated?</item>
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> They are calculated by multiplying the side lengths after partitioning the sides of the big rectangle. Like in 6•18, the partial products were 6•10 and 6•8. 


<para>
    </para><item>In an area model, what are two different ways the total area could get calculated?</item>
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> One way is by partitioning the sides to get smaller rectangles, finding the areas of those, and adding them up. Another way is by adding up all of the square units of the large rectangle. 


<para>
    </para><item>How do the partial products relate to the total area of the area model? </item>
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The partial products add up to the total area of the area model. Circulate around the room to hear similarities between groups. Write student ideas on the board to give value to contributions.  

</list>


<para><emphasis>Use the following questions to lead a class discussion.</emphasis></para>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item>Did anyone show the yellow boxes? What did they do? </item>
  <item>If you were to add up the areas inside the rectangle, where is a useful place to break up the rectangle so you have “easier” numbers to add up? </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Using an area model with numbers before variables allows students to connect their understanding of the area of a rectangle to algebraic thinking, namely the distributive property. When we multiply two numbers, we are essentially finding the area of a rectangle with a length and a width that are equivalent to those two factors. Since we will be talking about multiplying algebraic expressions, we could apply the same thinking as we do with the area of a rectangle. Before we can do that, students need to have a solid understanding of the area of a rectangle as the product of two factors. Starting with whole numbers is a jumping off point for students to see the distributive property in action with partial products that make sense.</para> 

<para>If students are struggling at the start of this lesson, you could consider asking them to multiply 15✕18 by hand, and see what that process is like. Save their work and compare it with their area model. Which one makes more sense to them? </para>

</section>
